hi  hi this is steve  kaufman here i haven't done these for a  while so i thought i would talk a little  bit about language again  i'm going to speak in english and then i  might just cover some of the same ground  in maybe in  a couple of other languages in  subsequent uh  videos here  uh i was doing some research on the web  and uh i came across the fact that uh  mcgraw-hill  has just had their  tabe t-a-b-e  testing  format for  esl or english  approved by the  u.s department of education national  reporting service and so forth and so on  and so i clicked through some of the  links that were there and i came to  mcgraw-hill site and i see that  that they have that there are first of  all there are six levels uh in  tave  you know beginner low beginner high  beginner whatever there's very different  various different ways you can  call these levels but it's not uncommon  to divide  proficiency levels uh in in a language  into six levels the european  framework does that  um in canada we have a very complicated  thing called the 12 benchmarks and there  it's 12 levels but  whatever  but if you go to the mcgraw-hill website  you find that they are selling  uh books and cds for each of these  levels  eighty dollars or so for a book with cd  um  i know when i was in japan  in bookstores there that  sell books related to learning english  half the books that are on sale relate  to a test of some kind more than half  so testing is a huge part  of the whole language learning language  teaching industry  i i have no idea how much money is  involved but i'm sure it's in the  billions of dollars  i can just imagine how much time is  spent developing these tests  i am sure that the people who develop  the tests include all kinds of academic  and government officials so that they'll  have an inside track  on getting their tests  recognized and approved  there's so many different tests there's  toll and toefl and toeic and ielts and  now this tabe and others  more or less all duplicating each other  and i i i kind of have this feeling i've  spoken about testing before but  it to me it's all a colossal waste of  money  um  speaking is so natural it's such a  natural activity yes you have to have  tests for engineers you need all kinds  of certification if someone's going to  build a bridge  or or design a bridge or design a  building and there are  very clear you know objective standards  of in terms of the  the strength and the durability and the  flexibility and all these things that  can be accurately measured that are  physical properties of structures  and you have to have someone designing  a structure that is familiar with how  materials  function and their properties and so  forth and so on but speaking is just  communicating that's all we're trying to  do  there is no um  hey there's no danger the house is not  gonna fall down  uh there is no real  uh objective measurable uh some people  make grammar mistakes but communicate  very well some people might score very  highly on a grammar test and can't  communicate  uh some people require only a limited  amount of of communication ability some  jobs or situations require much more  furthermore you know what are you  measuring uh if a person is  is being tested based on his or her  ability to communicate because they're  about to be put into a situation where  they have to communicate  well  if you test them two months later after  they've been put in that situation they  will have already improved a great deal  and i know myself when i speak a  language sometimes i get things wrong  sometimes i get them right sometimes i  do better sometimes i do worse  um  i mean really  we add up all the time and money that's  put into developing these tests  administering these tests studying for  these tests  going to schools where you learn how to  do well on these tests  if you take that money and said all  right  what are we what do we want to know can  this person communicate well let's just  sit down and talk to them  let's we can talk over skype  via  the the webcam we can even have the  person send in  a five-minute discussion like i'm doing  right now in english send one in and the  language that you claim to speak  uh i i mean the cost of that is far far  less  and nor is it obvious that someone who's  tested and of course we test all the  time we test we test in schools we test  in grade five in grade six in grade  seven and the vast majority of kids  studying languages end up not speaking  the language so i i think that the the  effort and money that's put into testing  is  is just one aspect of the language  instruction  industry  that is  you know  spectacularly counterproductive  and  i don't know what the solution is i  guess the system is structured in such a  way that that's that's just what happens  and the experts say we need this and so  we do it  yeah  but i think all the people that were  behind the subprime  uh  lending fiasco in the united states  those were all experts too and they  built up this tremendous house of cards  that came crashing down  somewhere  hopefully common sense has to prevail  i mean i am quite fluent in japanese i  have never taken a test in japanese and  i have no desire to take a test in  japanese and  yes french i had tests at school but  when i really improved my french was  when i went to university in france and  i had to improve it in a hurry and then  my tests were not on the french language  per se but i had tests on the subjects  that i was writing about and i speak 10  languages now i had i had to write a  test in chinese but i would have learned  it without the test i i was motivated by  things that interested me and my  interest in the language now people are  going to say not everyone's interested  and therefore you have to test those  people who aren't interested  i say if you didn't get them to study  for the test if you tried to make it  more interesting for them a larger  number of people would end up able to  communicate in the language and the  biggest example of that is japan where  two million people a year write the  toeic test they have terrible scores on  average those people in japan want to  learn english do those people who just  want to pass the toy test never end up  being able to communicate very well so  i'm going to stop it there and then i'm  going to perhaps  put up another one where i will say  another one of these but i will say the  same thing  in japanese or similar things bye for

